    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "419cece2_9bf853f9",
        "filename": "chip/ish/power_mgt.c",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 639,
      "author": {
        "id": 1330845
      },
      "writtenOn": "2019-06-04T12:54:45Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Why even halt at all in the negative case? It seems appropriate here to return to the top of the loop and try for a new next_delay again.",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 638,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 639,
        "endChar": 18
      },
      "revId": "66bc80ef84e422fd3b637c8a214fb59aec0cf764",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "95c130b5_f09c0910",
        "filename": "chip/ish/power_mgt.c",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 639,
      "author": {
        "id": 1259838
      },
      "writtenOn": "2019-06-04T16:41:15Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Shouldn\u0027t be just make next_delay a uint32_t instead. There should not be a negative case. The negative case means that we are sleeping for a long time",
      "parentUuid": "419cece2_9bf853f9",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 638,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 639,
        "endChar": 18
      },
      "revId": "66bc80ef84e422fd3b637c8a214fb59aec0cf764",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "796781c7_6ed73ec4",
        "filename": "chip/ish/power_mgt.c",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 639,
      "author": {
        "id": 1330845
      },
      "writtenOn": "2019-06-04T17:25:39Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I get why we would want to halt... the last_deadline won\u0027t be updated until we get another interrupt anyway, so wait until we get another interrupt.\n\nHowever, I think there has to be a better way to solve this. Perhaps could we change the definition of __hw_clock_event_get to never return times less than get_time().le.lo? This would also allow us to use a uint32_t like Jett mentioned.",
      "parentUuid": "95c130b5_f09c0910",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 638,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 639,
        "endChar": 18
      },
      "revId": "66bc80ef84e422fd3b637c8a214fb59aec0cf764",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "c8368737_afbe9ea7",
        "filename": "chip/ish/power_mgt.c",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 639,
      "author": {
        "id": 1319706
      },
      "writtenOn": "2019-06-05T09:13:15Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Since hw event timer interrupt (irq 8) has low priority (low value of vector number), other high priority interrupts will deliver and handled at first when coming at the same time, and it\u0027s possible to switch to idle task at the end of this high priority interrupt\u0027s isr if no task (except idle task) is ready.\n\nThe uart debug print is the example, uart0 interrupt (irq 34) has higher priority than timer, uart isr was called at first, timer interrupt was delayed. And for print log, uart\u0027s isr only operate registers for output, not signal any events which can wakeup task into ready state, so at the end of uart isr, the idle task was switched, and get negative \u0027next_delay\u0027 value since timer interrupt was delayed.\n\nFor other higher priority interrupt cases, if it\u0027s isr will wakeup task into ready state, at the end of this isr, the wakeup-ed task will be switched but not the idle task, so the negative value issue in idle task should not happen.      \n\nIn practice, the most cases I observed is the uart interrupt (irq 34) caused this issue. I checked the negative value of \u0027next_delay\u0027, it\u0027s between -7us ~ -100us, it\u0027s means the delay time of timer interrupt is mostly less than 100us. \n\nI understand ecos is interrupt/event driven scheduled rots, according to current interrupts priority settings, \u0027next_delay\u0027 is possible to be negative value, and suggest keep \u0027int\u0027 type for it, so we can detect it. \n\nI don\u0027t think the negative value of \u0027next_delay\u0027 here is means for long time sleep. We can see the task_wait_event(int timeout_us) api, if ‘timeout_us’ is negative, it\u0027s means wait infinitely for events with no timeout. And checked it\u0027s implementation, for negative \u0027timeout_us\u0027, it\u0027s not scheduled timer. The idle task only need check the __hw_clock_event_get() to get the next scheduled time, if no one scheduled event timer, the __hw_clock_event_get() will get the initial value 0xffffffff of \u0027last_deadline\u0027 value in hwtimer driver, its means sleep a very long time. \n\nSo I think, if get negative value of \u0027next_delay\u0027 in idle task, we can\u0027t use it for sleep, it\u0027s just a race condition problem due to timer interrupt was delayed by high priority tasks and ecos\u0027s interrupt driven scheduling design. I don\u0027t think it\u0027s a big problem, just need ignore it (halt for next update). \n\nThe best solution to avoid negative value, I think we need make event timer interrupt have the highest priority.    \n    \nyou\u0027re the real ecos experts, so please correct me if my understanding is wrong :)",
      "parentUuid": "796781c7_6ed73ec4",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 638,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 639,
        "endChar": 18
      },
      "revId": "66bc80ef84e422fd3b637c8a214fb59aec0cf764",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "346e2ab4_8d4e7cb0",
        "filename": "chip/ish/power_mgt.c",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 639,
      "author": {
        "id": 1259838
      },
      "writtenOn": "2019-06-05T17:48:33Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I do think we should make the timer interrupt higher priority to reduce the chances of this happening",
      "parentUuid": "c8368737_afbe9ea7",
